From 9843a7b902381ba97798b4d49f6ebfea7940b49a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:08:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] connectd: keep peers that name no networks by default

cln-application sends no networks TLV, so dropping silent peers dropped
every StartOS and Umbrel dashboard. Reported by kwsantiago in review, and
it is a mistake rather than a tradeoff I made knowingly: I was thinking
about lnd nodes on the SHA256d chain, which have never sent the field, and
not about client applications that speak the wire protocol purely to reach
a node's RPC and have no reason to know what chain they are on.

The fix is not to make those clients send the TLV. That pushes chain
awareness into a UI that otherwise needs none, and it only fixes the
clients we happen to know about.

The better argument is against my own patch. The init drop was never the
isolation, only defence in depth. A peer from the SHA256d chain that
connects anyway still cannot open a channel, because open_channel carries
chain_hash, and still cannot get its gossip accepted, because
channel_announcement carries it too. What the drop buys is that the
failure happens earlier and more legibly. That is worth having available
and not worth dropping every dashboard for, so it becomes opt-in.

The option is renamed rather than inverted in place, because CLN names an
option for the action it takes (--disable-dns sets use_dns false) and
"--allow-peers-without-networks" defaulting to allowing would be a flag
that does nothing. Generated bindings, schema and docs follow the rename.

A peer that names other chains but not ours is still dropped, as before.
That case is unambiguous.
